<activity>
 <!--Question 21-->
<statement>
<p>Describe the strength, form, and direction of the scatterplot for female Wimbledon champions. Are there any unusual observations?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Females have a positive, linear, and moderately strong association. There are no unusual data points.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 22-->
<statement>
<p>Describe the strength, form, and direction of the scatterplot for male Wimbledon champions. Are there any 
  unusual observations?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Males have a positive, linear, and weak to moderately strong association. There are no unusual data points.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 23-->
<statement>
<p>What do you predict for the value of the correlation coefficient between height and year for female winners 
  only? Why? What do you predict for male winners?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary: Females have correlation coefficient of about 0.40 and males of about 0.30.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 24-->
<statement>
<p>The actual correlation coefficient for females is 0.40 and for males is 0.36. Explain to a nonstatistician 
  why the correlation coefficients are different within the male/female groups as compared to the overall correlation of 0.19 based on the pattern of dots in the scatterplot.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Both correlations will be greater than the overall correlation of 0.189 because there is less variability in heights within each sex (and the overall trend is more clear), but females will have a higher correlation coefficient than males because their scatter plot is closer to a linear form than is the male scatterplot.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<p>Note, however, that the strength of the association is not directly impacted by the size of the sample, 
  but by how closely the observations follow the (linear) pattern.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Steps 4 and 5: Draw inferences and formulate conclusions</title>
<p>In a later section you will explore how to evaluate whether a correlation is statistically significant, 
  but that's not necessarily needed/appropriate in this case. The data here represent the entire population 
  of Wimbledon winners (a census).
</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 25-->
<statement>
<p>How does the strength and direction of association compare between men and women? What does this tell 
  us about the pattern of height of Wimbledon winners over time?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Because both have moderately strong positive associations, there is a trend of increasing height in the champions for both men and women over time.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 26-->
<statement>
<p>Can we say that year causes changes in the height of the Wimbledon winners? Why or why not? Can we say 
  that being taller makes you more likely to win Wimbledon?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    We can't make any causal conclusions because this is observational data (no random assignment was used). Height is merely associated with the year.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Steps 6: Look back and ahead</title>
<activity>
 <!--Question 27-->
<statement>
<p>Name one additional variable you would like measured on each Wimbledon winner and
explain why.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary.  One example is:
  </p>
  <p> 
    Are they a previous Wimbledon champion or not? What is their nationality? These could be good predictors of height.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 28-->
<statement>
<p>Describe how you could do a similar study for another sport. What two variables would you collect? 
  What would you expect to see? Why? What third variable could also be measured and be important to consider?</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary.  One example:
  </p>
  <p>
    Use the sport of golf where the observational units are the PGA champions for each year and you also collect the height of the champion each year. Sex could be used as a third variable again as there are both male and female PGA winners each year.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>